  "reason": "Evidence: RNA is the dominant modality (rna_only acc=0.5907 vs image_only acc=0.3316), and the best multimodal config (config_020, acc=0.6352) uses pca_n2000 + imagenet_vit_l + avgpool + weighted. We now know avgpool+weighted is superior to transformer+weighted (0.6352 vs 0.5856) and weighted beats concat (0.6352 vs 0.6287). Uncertainty: We have only tested one RNA encoder (pca_n2000) in the best-performing pipeline. Since RNA drives most of the signal, the RNA encoder choice is the highest-leverage untested design decision. Question: Does a learned variational RNA representation (scvi_n200) outperform a linear PCA reduction (pca_n2000) when all other components (imagenet_vit_l, avgpool, weighted, multiprofile) are held at their best-known settings? Why this experiment: config_007 is a clean single-variable swap against config_020 (only rna_encoder changes), isolating the RNA encoder effect. This is more informative than testing the image encoder (resnet50) because RNA contributes far more to performance, so understanding whether a learned encoder can push the RNA signal higher is the key question for improving the overall model under a limited budget."
}

